2. Personal and noncommercial use

You may copy, run and modify the Software without charge or a time limit for your own noncommercial personal projects, learning, research and hobbies.

You may also use the Software without charge or a time limit to develop, maintain or contribute to bona fide noncommercial open-source projects. For this purpose, an open-source project is one made available under an Open Source Initiative-approved license. This permission covers using Raven as a tool for that work; it does not allow relicensing Raven itself under an incompatible license.

These permissions do not cover Commercial Use as defined below. An individual’s unrelated personal or volunteer activity does not become Commercial Use merely because the individual is employed by a business.

3. Commercial use

“Commercial Use” means use of the Software by, on behalf of, or for the operations, products or services of a business operated for profit. This includes sole proprietors, freelancers, consultants, startups and other for-profit organizations.

Commercial Use includes internal business use, production use, work for customers, paid services and development of a business’s products. It applies whether or not the business currently earns revenue or makes a profit.

Publishing a project’s source code or using an open-source license for that project does not exempt otherwise commercial activity. Work performed by or for a for-profit business remains Commercial Use.

“Organization” means the business for which Commercial Use occurs and its affiliates under common control. “Control” means ownership of more than half of the voting interests or the power to direct management. Commercial Use by employees, contractors or other people acting for an Organization is use by that Organization.

4. Commercial trial

You and your Organization may copy, run and modify the Software for Commercial Use without charge for one period of 90 consecutive calendar days.

The trial begins on the first day that you, or anyone acting for your Organization, runs, deploys or modifies any version of the Software offered under this license for Commercial Use. The first day counts as day one.

The trial includes internal business use and production use. Merely viewing the public source code or making a repository fork without running, deploying or modifying it for Commercial Use does not start the trial.

An Organization has one shared trial. Different people, teams, projects, machines, accounts, deployments or affiliates do not receive separate trials. Reinstalling, copying, modifying, forking or upgrading the Software does not restart or extend the trial. Incorporating or reorganizing substantially the same business does not create a new trial.

5. Commercial use after the trial

After the trial expires, Commercial Use requires a separate, valid paid team license from the Licensors covering that use. If you do not have that license, you must stop Commercial Use.

The price, covered Organization, users, duration and other commercial terms must be agreed separately with the Licensors. This license does not automatically enroll anyone in a subscription, authorize a charge or create a payment obligation merely because the trial ends.

Expiry of the commercial trial does not end otherwise permitted personal or noncommercial use or the source-sharing permissions below.

